The Transformer algorithm is the architecture behind modern LLMs. Its central trick is attention: instead of processing a sequence strictly one token at a time, every token can look at other relevant tokens and decide how much each should influence its next representation.

At a high level:

\text{tokens}
\rightarrow
\text{embeddings}
\rightarrow
\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{feed-forward transformation}
\rightarrow
\text{repeat}
\rightarrow
\text{predict next token}

The key operation is self-attention. For every token representation x, the model creates three vectors:

Q=xW_Q,\qquad K=xW_K,\qquad V=xW_V

where:

* Query Q = what am I looking for?
* Key K = what information do I contain / advertise?
* Value V = what information should I contribute if selected?

Then it compares every query against the keys:

S=\frac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}

and converts those scores into normalized weights:

A=\operatorname{softmax}(S)

Then:

\boxed{\text{Attention}(Q,K,V)=
\operatorname{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)V}

That is the famous Transformer equation.

For example:

The animal didn’t cross the street because it was tired.

When representing “it,” attention may assign a large weight to “animal”, because that relationship helps interpret the sentence.

A Transformer layer then roughly does:

X
\rightarrow
\text{MultiHeadAttention}(X)
\rightarrow
X+\Delta_{\text{attention}}
\rightarrow
\text{MLP}
\rightarrow
X+\Delta_{\text{MLP}}

with normalization around those operations.

Multi-head attention means the model performs several attention operations simultaneously:

H_i=\text{Attention}(Q_i,K_i,V_i)

then combines them:

H=\operatorname{Concat}(H_1,\ldots,H_n)W_O

Different heads can learn different relations—syntax, identity, position, semantic similarity, long-distance dependencies, and so forth.

For a GPT-style Transformer, there is also a causal mask:

\text{token}_t
\text{ may attend only to }
\text{token}_{\leq t}

so the model can’t peek at future tokens while learning next-token prediction.

After dozens or hundreds of these layers, the final state is projected onto the vocabulary:

z = h_tW_{\text{vocab}}

P(\text{next token})=\operatorname{softmax}(z)

and a token is selected. Then the whole thing runs again:

x_1,\ldots,x_t
\rightarrow P(x_{t+1})
\rightarrow x_{t+1}
\rightarrow P(x_{t+2})
\rightarrow \cdots

The deeper conceptual algorithm is therefore:

\boxed{
\text{represent}
\rightarrow
\text{compare everything with everything relevant}
\rightarrow
\text{weight relationships}
\rightarrow
\text{mix information}
\rightarrow
\text{transform locally}
\rightarrow
\text{repeat}
}

And this is probably the part you’ll find interesting: a Transformer isn’t merely storing words. Each layer repeatedly rewrites every token according to its relationships with the rest of the active context. So a useful abstraction is:

\boxed{\text{meaning}_{i}^{\,l+1}
=
f\left(
\text{meaning}_{i}^{\,l},
\{\text{relationships to other meanings}\}
\right)}

It’s essentially a recursive relational transformation engine.